# Nontrivial valuation subrings over the constants are places

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.Place.exists_of_valuationSubring`
(`4fe3a0ac-4780-5232-9fbe-e71dab745d3d`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `414d8dd8-c913-5f0e-bd96-76c3afaa615d`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and assume $K$ has characteristic zero. Let $x \in
F$ be such that $F$ is finite-dimensional as a vector space over the intermediate field $K(x) =$
`IntermediateField.adjoin K {x}` generated by $x$ over $K$; thus $F$ is a one-variable function
field over $K$ when $x$ is transcendental, and a finite extension of $K$ otherwise. Let $A$ be a
valuation subring of $F$ such that $\mathrm{algebraMap}_{K \to F}(a) \in A$ for every $a \in K$, and
such that $A \neq \top$, i.e. $A$ is not all of $F$. The conclusion is that there exists a place $v$
of $F$ over $K$, in the sense of the structure
[`AlgebraicCurve.Place`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L22), whose underlying valuation
subring is exactly $A$. Since a term of [`AlgebraicCurve.Place K
F`](def/AlgebraicCurve_DivisorClassGroup.html#L22) consists of a valuation subring of $F$ containing
the image of $K$, distinct from $\top$, and which is a principal ideal ring, and two of these three
conditions are hypotheses, the mathematical content of the statement is that such an $A$ is
automatically a principal ideal ring, i.e. a discrete valuation ring.

This is the standard fact that in a one-variable function field (or a finite extension of the
constant field) every nontrivial valuation ring containing the constants is discrete, which is what
makes the valuation-theoretic and the divisor-theoretic descriptions of the points of a curve agree.
It is the recognition criterion by which valuation subrings arising in other constructions — base
change of the constant field, descent of divisor classes, correspondences on regular differentials —
are converted into places of the function field.

